# Key Takeaways

This lab demonstrates how **null byte injection** can bypass filename validation mechanisms.

Although the application expected an image file ending in `.png`, the injected null byte caused the underlying filesystem to truncate the string before the extension, allowing arbitrary file access.

While modern programming languages and frameworks generally reject null bytes in file paths, this vulnerability remains relevant when auditing legacy applications, native extensions, or software built on C-based APIs.
